---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'

import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/base.astro'
import RouteBar from '@/components/site/nav/route-bar.astro'
import SectionNav from '@/components/site/section-nav/section-nav.astro'
import RouteFoot from '@/components/site/case-study/route-foot.astro'
import { getLinkTargetAttributes } from '@/lib/links'
import installTranscript from '@/assets/projects/aitk.png'

const artifactLinks = [
  { label: 'GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/erclx/aitk' },
  { label: 'npm', href: 'https://www.npmjs.com/package/@erclx/aitk' },
]

const navItems = [
  { id: 'problem', label: 'problem' },
  { id: 'thesis', label: 'thesis' },
  { id: 'taxonomy', label: 'taxonomy' },
  { id: 'model', label: 'model' },
  { id: 'agents', label: 'agents' },
]

const operations = [
  {
    name: 'install',
    behavior: 'Overwrites. Runs once to bootstrap a project.',
  },
  {
    name: 'sync',
    behavior: 'Updates only files already present. Safe to re-run on a cadence.',
  },
  {
    name: 'build',
    behavior: 'Derives a payload from current state for pasting into any chat.',
  },
]

const contentKinds = [
  { kind: 'configs', rule: 'overwrite' },
  { kind: 'seeds', rule: 'merge additively, survive user edits' },
  { kind: 'references', rule: 'copy through an extends chain' },
  { kind: 'generated', rule: 'derive from target state' },
]

const prescriptions = [
  'Give the agent a way to pull in its own context from code, wikis, and discussions',
  'Break work into loops and routines',
  'Let the agent start other agents',
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="aitk · Eric Le"
  description="One set of agent rules, skills, and standards installed into every project and kept in step. The install taxonomy, the adoption thesis, and the operating model behind it."
  surface="case-study"
>
  <SectionNav items={navItems} instant />

  <RouteBar here="aitk" />

  <main>
    <section class="page-inset pt-16 pb-12 md:pt-20 md:pb-16">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <p data-fade class="text-muted-foreground text-label tracking-wide uppercase">
          Project
        </p>
        <h1
          data-fade          class="font-display text-foreground mt-4 text-[2.25rem] leading-tight font-semibold md:text-[2.75rem]"
        >
          aitk
        </h1>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-lede mt-4"
        >
          A CLI that installs one set of agent rules, skills, and standards into
          every project and keeps them in step.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-3"
        >
          Public and MIT licensed. Installs from npm as
          <code class="font-mono">@erclx/aitk</code> and as a Claude Code
          plugin.
        </p>
        <ul
          data-fade          class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6"
        >
          {
            artifactLinks.map((link) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={link.href}
                  {...getLinkTargetAttributes(link.href)}
                  class="text-foreground text-label inline-flex min-h-11 min-w-11 items-center underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
                >
                  {link.label}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        {/* The still closes the opening rather than interrupting it, so a
            reader meets the artifact existing before the argument starts. */}
        <figure
          data-fade          class="figure-breakout bg-card border-border/60 mt-12 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border"
        >
          <Image
            src={installTranscript}
            alt="A terminal installing aitk as a Claude Code plugin in three commands, ending with the plugin listed as enabled"
            class="block h-auto w-full"
            loading="eager"
          />
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="problem" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          problem
        </h2>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6"
        >
          Every project I started got the same agent scaffolding typed into it
          again, and every copy moved away from the last. I wanted one source I
          could install rather than a folder I kept re-authoring.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          Every repository re-authors the same agent scaffolding. The rules an
          agent reads, the skills it runs, the prose standards it writes
          against, the tooling config it verifies with. Each copy starts from
          the last one and drifts from it, so the same agent gets different
          instructions depending on which checkout it opened.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          Drift on this surface is quiet. A rule that fell behind still loads,
          still reads as authoritative, and produces work against a convention
          the team abandoned two projects ago. Nothing fails, so nothing
          reports it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="thesis" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          thesis
        </h2>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6"
        >
          This is infrastructure that carries a team across a step of AI
          adoption, rather than tooling one engineer prefers. Four prescriptions
          are published for the gap between running agents ad hoc and running
          them as a system. Three of them are what this installs.
        </p>

        <ul
          data-fade          class="mt-10 flex flex-col gap-3"
        >
          {
            prescriptions.map((item) => (
              <li class="text-foreground text-body border-border/60 border-l-2 pl-4">
                {item}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-8"
        >
          The fourth is raising agency and review speed once agents touch code
          other teams own, which is an organizational permission rather than a
          thing a CLI can install.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          The claim stops there deliberately, with the token budget named out
          loud as the ceiling. A larger claim about autonomous fleets is one
          question about agent count away from falling over.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="taxonomy" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          taxonomy
        </h2>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6"
        >
          Content reaches a project through three separate operations, and what
          each one is allowed to touch is the whole design.
        </p>

        <dl
          data-fade          class="mt-10 grid gap-y-2 sm:grid-cols-[6rem_1fr]"
        >
          {
            operations.map((row) => (
              <div class="contents font-mono text-label">
                <dt class="text-foreground">{row.name}</dt>
                <dd class="text-muted-foreground">{row.behavior}</dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>

        <figure
          data-fade          class="figure-breakout bg-card mt-10 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg p-6 md:p-10"
        >
          <figcaption class="text-muted-foreground font-mono text-label mb-4">
            content kinds and their merge rule
          </figcaption>
          <dl class="grid gap-y-2 sm:grid-cols-[8rem_1fr]">
            {
              contentKinds.map((row) => (
                <div class="contents font-mono text-label">
                  <dt class="text-muted-foreground">{row.kind}</dt>
                  <dd class="text-foreground">{row.rule}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </figure>

        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-8"
        >
          That taxonomy is what makes an update safe to run without reading it
          first. A sync that only touches files already present cannot introduce
          a surface the project never asked for, and a seed that merges rather
          than replaces cannot discard the edits a team made to it. The cost of
          pulling the toolkit forward drops to a command, which is the only
          version of this a team actually runs.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          Stacks compose through the same <code class="font-mono">extends</code>
          field. <code class="font-mono">react</code> extends
          <code class="font-mono">node</code>, which extends
          <code class="font-mono">base</code>, and installing one pulls
          everything it inherits. Rules carry a path glob, so a Python rule
          stays dormant in a TypeScript project rather than adding noise the
          agent has to filter.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="model" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          model
        </h2>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6"
        >
          The toolkit ships the way of working alongside the files. Sessions
          split by vantage rather than by capability.
        </p>

        <figure
          data-fade          class="figure-breakout bg-card mt-10 rounded-lg p-6 md:p-10"
        >
          <div class="font-mono text-label flex flex-col items-center gap-6">
            <div class="border-border text-foreground rounded-md border px-4 py-2">
              orchestrator, warm
            </div>
            <div aria-hidden="true" class="bg-border h-6 w-px"></div>
            <div aria-hidden="true" class="bg-border h-px w-3/4"></div>
            <div class="grid w-full grid-cols-1 gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
              <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-3">
                <div aria-hidden="true" class="bg-border -mt-8 h-6 w-px"></div>
                <div class="text-muted-foreground tracking-wide uppercase">
                  plans and reviews
                </div>
                <div
                  class="border-border text-foreground rounded-md border px-4 py-3 text-center"
                >
                  holds the cross-feature picture, edits no tracked file
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-3">
                <div aria-hidden="true" class="bg-border -mt-8 h-6 w-px"></div>
                <div class="text-muted-foreground tracking-wide uppercase">
                  workers, cold
                </div>
                <div
                  class="border-border text-foreground rounded-md border px-4 py-3 text-center"
                >
                  one plan each in its own worktree, stopping at the pull
                  request
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-8"
        >
          Review travels on the pull request itself. One skill posts findings to
          it, another reads them back, fixes each, and pushes a follow-up, and
          the review then runs again against only the commits that follow-up
          added. The heading carries the state, so a reader knows whether
          anything is open without reading the thread.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          The two passes differ by vantage rather than by depth. A worker's
          self-review asks whether it built the plan and whether it passes, and
          it is blind to its own misreadings, because the same misreading wrote
          both the code and the review. The orchestrator's review is a fresh
          session carrying every sibling branch in flight, so it can question
          the plan itself.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          What binds the number of tracks is collision between file sets rather
          than a fixed cap. A candidate opens only where its files are disjoint
          from every track already running, and what thins as tracks multiply is
          the review attention each one gets.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="agents" class="page-inset py-16 md:py-20">
      <div class="mx-auto prose-column">
        <h2 data-fade class="text-foreground font-display text-2xl leading-tight font-semibold md:text-3xl">
          agents
        </h2>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-foreground text-lede mt-6"
        >
          Every command has a non-interactive path, every listing emits JSON on
          stdout, and the framed terminal UI goes to stderr. A skill shelling
          out to the CLI can treat stdout as data without parsing around a
          progress frame.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          Because <code class="font-mono">bash exec</code> drops parent traps,
          each subcommand re-arms its exit trap before an early JSON exit, so
          exactly one frame renders per invocation.
        </p>
        <p
          data-fade          class="text-muted-foreground text-body mt-6"
        >
          Verification runs on sandbox scenarios rather than unit tests. Each
          scenario provisions an isolated project state anchored to a baseline
          git ref, and a disposable GitHub remote lets the skills that depend on
          the GitHub CLI run end to end, including opening a pull request,
          merging it, and editing its body. Naming a scenario through an
          environment variable routes it non-interactively, so CI and an agent
          exercise the same path a person would.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  <RouteFoot />

  <script>
    import { initWayHome } from '@/components/site/case-study/way-home'
    import { initReveal } from '@/lib/reveal'

    initReveal()
    initWayHome()
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
